L = {}
L["lions-den"] = """Daniel was now an old man, and he had served under several kings. When Darius the Mede took over Babylon, he put Daniel in charge of the other high officials, because Daniel had an excellent spirit and the king planned to set him over the whole kingdom. The other officials were jealous. They looked for something to use against him, but they couldn't find any corruption or negligence. He was trustworthy. So they said to one another, we will never find any basis for charges against Daniel unless it has something to do with the law of his God.

They went to the king and persuaded him to sign a decree. For thirty days, anyone who prayed to any god or man except the king would be thrown into the den of lions. Once the king signed it, the law of the Medes and Persians could not be changed.

Daniel heard that the decree had been signed. He went home to his upstairs room, where the windows opened toward Jerusalem. Three times a day he got down on his knees and prayed and gave thanks to his God, just as he had always done. He didn't hide it or close the windows. The officials were waiting, and they found him praying and asking God for help.

They went to the king and reminded him of the decree. Then they told him that Daniel was ignoring it. The king was deeply distressed. He liked Daniel and made every effort until sundown to find a way to rescue him. But the men reminded him that no decree could be changed. At last the king gave the order, and Daniel was brought and thrown into the lions' den. The king said to him, may your God, whom you serve continually, rescue you. A stone was placed over the mouth of the den, and the king sealed it with his own ring.

Then the king went back to his palace and spent the night fasting. He refused entertainment and couldn't sleep. At the first light of dawn, he got up and hurried to the lions' den. When he came near, he called out in an anguished voice, Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to rescue you from the lions?

Daniel answered, may the king live forever. My God sent his angel, and he shut the mouths of the lions. They have not hurt me, because I was found innocent in his sight. Nor have I ever done any wrong before you, Your Majesty. The king was overjoyed and gave orders to lift Daniel out of the den. When he was lifted out, no wound was found on him, because he had trusted in his God.

Why does this matter? Daniel's habit is the key. He didn't start praying when the law was passed. He had been praying three times a day for years, and when the pressure came, he simply kept going. Faith that holds in a crisis is usually built in ordinary days.

What happened after? The men who plotted against Daniel faced the consequences of their own plan. King Darius then wrote to everyone in his kingdom that they should fear and reverence the God of Daniel, for he is the living God and he endures forever. Daniel continued to prosper through the reigns of Darius and Cyrus.

What does this mean for us today? Most of us won't face lions, but we all face pressure to hide our faith or to drop our habits of prayer. Daniel's example is to keep praying, quietly and steadily. It also shows that God can protect his people, and that even when he doesn't change the situation, he is present in it.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is the living God who rescues those who trust him. And it points to Jesus, who was also sealed behind a stone with a royal seal, and who came out alive."""

L["jonah-whale"] = """God spoke to a prophet named Jonah and gave him a job. Go to the great city of Nineveh and preach against it, because its wickedness has come up before me. Nineveh was the capital of Assyria, Israel's feared enemy. Jonah didn't want to go. He didn't want the Ninevites to repent and be forgiven, because he thought they deserved punishment. So he ran the other way. He went down to the port of Joppa, found a ship headed to Tarshish, which was in the opposite direction, paid the fare, and went below deck to get away from the Lord.

Then the Lord sent a great wind on the sea, and such a violent storm arose that the ship threatened to break up. The sailors were terrified, and each cried out to his own god. They threw cargo overboard to lighten the ship. Meanwhile, Jonah was below deck, sound asleep. The captain found him and said, how can you sleep? Get up and call on your god! Perhaps he will take notice of us, and we will not perish.

The sailors cast lots to find out who was responsible for the storm, and the lot fell on Jonah. They asked him who he was. He said, I am a Hebrew and I worship the Lord, the God of heaven, who made the sea and the dry land. The men were even more afraid. What have you done? they asked. The storm was getting worse. Jonah told them, pick me up and throw me into the sea, and it will become calm. I know that it is my fault that this great storm has come upon you.

The sailors didn't want to do it. They tried to row back to land, but they couldn't. Finally, they prayed to the Lord, asking him not to hold them accountable for taking an innocent life. Then they picked up Jonah and threw him overboard, and the raging sea grew calm. The men were filled with awe of the Lord, and they offered a sacrifice and made vows to him.

But the Lord had arranged for a huge fish to swallow Jonah, and Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights. From inside the fish, Jonah prayed. In my distress I called to the Lord, and he answered me. From deep in the realm of the dead I called for help, and you listened to my cry. You hurled me into the depths, into the very heart of the seas, and the currents swirled about me. When my life was ebbing away, I remembered you, Lord. What I have vowed I will make good. Salvation comes from the Lord.

And the Lord commanded the fish, and it vomited Jonah onto dry land.

Why does this matter? Jonah tried to run from God, and the more he ran, the lower he went. Down to Joppa, down into the ship, down into the sea. But God didn't give up on him. Even the storm and the fish were part of God's mercy, because they brought Jonah back. And the sailors, who were not Israelites, ended up worshiping the Lord. God was already reaching out to outsiders, even through a runaway prophet.

What happened after? God spoke to Jonah a second time. Go to the great city of Nineveh. This time Jonah went. Jesus later said that just as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the huge fish, so the Son of Man would be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth. Jonah's story was a sign pointing to the resurrection.

What does this mean for us today? We all know what it is like to run from something God is asking us to do. The story says that God is persistent, and that he doesn't throw us away when we fail. It also shows that the way back can start with a prayer, even from the bottom. Jonah prayed from the belly of a fish, and God heard him.

That is why this story is so important. It says that you can't outrun God's mercy. And it points to Jesus, who went down into death for us and came back up on the third day."""

L["nineveh"] = """God gave Jonah a second chance. The word of the Lord came to Jonah a second time. Go to the great city of Nineveh and proclaim to it the message I give you. This time Jonah obeyed. He went to Nineveh, which was so large that it took three days to go through it.

On the first day, Jonah began to walk into the city, and he gave a short and blunt message. Forty more days and Nineveh will be overthrown. That was all. He didn't say anything about God's mercy or how they could be saved. It is possible that he hoped they wouldn't listen.

But the Ninevites believed God. They declared a fast, and all of them, from the greatest to the least, put on rough cloth. When the news reached the king of Nineveh, he got up from his throne, took off his royal robes, covered himself with rough cloth, and sat down in the dust. He issued a decree. By the decree of the king and his nobles, no one, not even animals, may taste anything. Everyone must call urgently on God. Let them give up their evil ways and their violence. Who knows? God may yet relent with compassion and turn from his fierce anger so that we will not perish.

When God saw what they did and how they turned from their evil ways, he relented and did not bring on them the destruction he had threatened.

You might expect Jonah to celebrate. But he was furious. He prayed to the Lord, and his prayer reveals why he ran in the first place. Isn't this what I said, Lord, when I was still at home? That is why I was so quick to flee to Tarshish. I knew that you are a gracious and compassionate God, slow to anger and abounding in love, a God who relents from sending calamity. Jonah knew exactly who God was, and he resented it. He asked God to take his life.

God asked him a question. Is it right for you to be angry? Jonah went out and sat down east of the city to see what would happen. The Lord God provided a leafy plant and made it grow up over Jonah to give him shade. Jonah was very happy about the plant. But the next day, God sent a worm that chewed the plant so that it withered. When the sun rose, God sent a scorching wind, and Jonah grew faint. He said, it would be better for me to die than to live.

God asked, is it right for you to be angry about the plant? Jonah said, yes, it is. I am so angry I wish I were dead. Then the Lord said, you have been concerned about this plant, though you did not tend it or make it grow. It sprang up overnight and died overnight. And should I not have concern for the great city of Nineveh, in which there are more than a hundred and twenty thousand people who cannot tell their right hand from their left, and also many animals?

The book ends there, with a question. It never says how Jonah answered.

Why does this matter? Nineveh was not a friendly place. The Assyrians were known for cruelty. And yet God cared about them, and when they repented, he forgave them. It was Jonah, the prophet, who had the hard heart. The story challenges anyone who thinks God's love is only for people like us.

What happened after? Nineveh's repentance lasted for a while, and later Assyria returned to its old ways. Jesus said that the people of Nineveh will stand up at the judgment and condemn the people who rejected him, because they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and now something greater than Jonah is here.

What does this mean for us today? Is there someone you think is too far gone to be forgiven? Jonah's story says that God's mercy is bigger than our list of who deserves it. It also asks whether we are glad when God forgives someone we don't like. It is easy to love mercy when it is for us.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is gracious and compassionate, and that his love reaches everyone who turns to him. And it points to Jesus, who came to seek and save the lost, including our enemies."""
